<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">4 July in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 4 July, from 1187 to 1997.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1187">1187 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">839 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Saladin destroys the Crusader army at the Battle of Hattin</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> destroyed the army of the Crusader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> and captured its king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>. The victory allowed Saladin to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> later that year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1456">1456 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">570 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sultan Mehmed II begins the siege of Belgrade</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Ottoman forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> began the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, then held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and crucial for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. The siege lasted until 22 July, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> relieved the city.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-hungarian-wars">Ottoman–Hungarian wars</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1776">1776 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">250 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Continental Congress adopts the Declaration of Independence</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress approved the final text of the Declaration, drafted chiefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>. Its assertion that all men are created equal, with unalienable rights, became the founding statement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1802">1802 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">224 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">US Military Academy opens at West Point</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Military Academy formally opened at West Point, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. It went on to train generations of officers, among them Grant, Lee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> and MacArthur.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1826">1826 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">200 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John Adams and Thomas Jefferson die on the same day</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Former presidents John Adams and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> died within hours of each other on the 50th anniversary of the Declaration. Adams's reported last words were 'Thomas Jefferson survives', unaware his rival had died first.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1863">1863 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">163 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vicksburg surrenders to Ulysses S. Grant</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After a 47-day siege, John Pemberton surrendered Vicksburg and nearly 30,000 men. With Port Hudson falling days later, the Union controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and split the Confederacy in two.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1943">1943 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">83 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Polish leader Władysław Sikorski dies in a crash at Gibraltar</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">General Sikorski, prime minister of the Polish government-in-exile, was killed when his aircraft crashed seconds after take-off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. His death removed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>' most respected Polish voice at a critical moment.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1946">1946 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">80 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Philippines gains independence from the United States</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> became a sovereign republic after nearly half a century of American rule, as promised by the 1934 Tydings-McDuffie Act. For decades 4 July was celebrated as its Independence Day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1997">1997 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">29 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mars Pathfinder lands on Mars</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">NASA's Pathfinder bounced to a landing on airbags in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> Vallis and soon deployed Sojourner, the first rover on another planet. The mission returned thousands of images and revived public interest in Mars.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 4 July <span class="n">2</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Giuseppe Garibaldi</span><span class="tp">1807–1882</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edi-rama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edi-rama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edi Rama</span><span class="tp">b. 1964</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 4 July <span class="n">9</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Taejo of Goryeo</span><span class="tp">877–943</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gwangjong-of-goryeo"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Gwangjong of Goryeo</span><span class="tp">925–975</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/raynald-of-chatillon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Raynald of Châtillon</span><span class="tp">c. 1125–1187</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Carlo I Tocco</span><span class="tp">d. 1429</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pedro-de-alvarado.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pedro de Alvarado</span><span class="tp">c. 1485–1541</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/samuel-richardson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/samuel-richardson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Samuel Richardson</span><span class="tp">1689–1761</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thomas-jefferson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Thomas Jefferson</span><span class="tp">1743–1826</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/poundmaker"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/poundmaker.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Poundmaker</span><span class="tp">c. 1842–1886</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/swami-vivekananda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Swami Vivekananda</span><span class="tp">1863–1902</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
